Resource snapshot and change publication — implementation plan
==============================================================

Source proposal: [Rendering.ResourceChangePublication.Proposal.md](Rendering.ResourceChangePublication.Proposal.md).
Parent work: Rendering.GpuCommands.todo, Phase 9.2. Prerequisite: its existing-work boundary, Phase 9.1.
Status: approved design; implementation not started. Every task below is open.

Goal
  Establish one manager-owned completion contract for snapshot delivery and meaningful resource changes
  across all fourteen resource kinds. Preserve original identities, ownership, context affinity, query
  policy, executed-state suppression and explicit owner events while removing split notification routes.

Controlling documents
  The source proposal governs every phase. Preserve the applicable authority, resource and submission
  contracts in Rendering.GpuCommands.Contracts.md, Rendering.GpuResources.md,
  Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md (Prepared resource submission), and Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md.
  Reconcile these documents with the approved refinement rather than retaining superseded notification
  behavior. Rendering.GpuCommands.Migrations.md records implementation routes and qualification limits.

Execution boundaries
  - Complete the producer/consumer migration as one implementation unit in Phase 2. Do not introduce
    forwarding shims, parallel event systems, temporary old/new dispatch routes or deferred caller migration.
    Within that unit, implement prerequisites before consumers, but do not claim partial source closure.
  - Keep RHIResourceManager authoritative; no new resource hierarchy, native-ID registry, public event bus,
    persistent context-local registry or generation token. ResourceChangeKind is an enum without an RHI prefix.
  - Facts remain derived from established command outcomes, defaults and authorized existing reads.
    Add no native queries to detect changes, no requested/actual duplicate cache and no ambient error drain.
  - Content writes and GPU completion remain outside metadata notifications. Keep backend binding/state
    knowledge and eligible command suppression separate from canonical publication.
  - Run builds/tests through subagents. Use CPU-only tests for manager logic and focused GPU integration
    only where native behavior is required. No bulk pixel readback, full-suite timing exercise or benchmarks.
    Full integrated/package/live qualification stays in parent Phase 9.3; suite cleanup stays in Phase 10.
  - Preserve staged and unrelated user changes. This plan does not authorize staging or commits.

Phase 1: Establish the complete migration and behavior map
  Outcome: every existing producer, consumer and revision dependency has a specified final route.

  ☐ Trace GpuResourceMetadataEntry.Publish/Associate, GpuResource<T>.HandleMetadataChanged,
    RHIResourceManager.MetadataPublication, MetadataOperation, MetadataObservation and ProgramFields.
    Map creation, owned composition, intercepted mutation, explicit discovery/recovery and local delivery
    to the proposal's common completion path, including initially reserved and newly associated resources.
  ☐ Trace NotifyImageDependents, NotifyProgramDependents, NotifyFramebufferDependents, OwnerImages,
    texture-view availability, buffer/renderbuffer-backed textures and deletion dependencies.
    Record actual recipients, weak ownership, projection order, duplicate-delivery avoidance and failures.
    Distinguish currently proven defects from separate routes that already serve legitimate responsibilities.
  ☐ Map every resource kind to its canonical struct and change consumers using the proposal's coverage
    table. Explicitly include Buffer, Texture, Renderbuffer, Framebuffer, Shader, Program, VertexArray,
    TransformFeedback, Sampler, ProgramPipeline, Query, Sync, Semaphore and MemoryObject.
    Treat GpuImageMetadata as a projection; texture variants remain in the Texture family.
  ☐ Inventory MetadataRevision, StorageRevision and executable-generation readers, pending effect flags,
    admission/reentry guards, retirement paths and off-thread disposal handoffs. Identify any consumer
    that currently treats republication as a change or depends on the old revision increment policy.
  ☐ Map explicit framebuffer resize/rebuild/import owner events and initiating-owner suppression separately
    from canonical changes. Trace replaced-owner images, borrowed aliases, native views and name reuse.
  ☐ Record the implementation-to-requirement mapping concisely in Rendering.GpuCommands.Migrations.md:
    current route -> replacement owner -> preserved behavior -> focused verification. No separate audit
    report is required. Confirm the proposed order can migrate all coupled paths without a compatibility layer.
  ☐ Completion: all families, producers, recipients and revision readers are mapped; no unresolved
    interpretation prevents implementing the approved contract. No production change is required here.

Phase 2: Replace publication and notification as one complete unit
  Depends on Phase 1.
  Outcome: all resource mutations and lifetime transitions use the approved completion contract.

  ☐ Implement canonical value equality and matching hashing for collection-bearing GpuTextureMetadata,
    GpuFramebufferMetadata and GpuProgramMetadata. Compare all scalar facts and backing identities;
    ignore immutable allocation identity and dictionary insertion order. Match texture images by mip/face,
    program resources by interface/index and unit assignments by location. Preserve the distinction between
    default/unavailable and known empty collections. Remove special HasSameConfiguration-style alternatives.
  ☐ Introduce internal RHIResourceChange and ResourceChangeKind with Metadata, Storage, Executable,
    Availability and Retired reasons, resulting availability and existing revision values. Associate delivery
    with the original registration. Keep family-specific reactions behind one internal resource callback;
    wrappers without derived work need no handler or independent subscription system.
  ☐ Classify candidates against last committed facts, prior externally visible availability and operation
    evidence. Initial complete publication reports metadata/availability; identical republication advances
    no revision and emits no change. Still assign complete snapshots to newly associated/missing recipients.
    Mere operation exclusion must not generate unavailable/available event pairs on successful commands.
  ☐ Preserve explicit Storage/Executable effects when descriptors/interfaces compare equal. Preserve uncertain
    accumulated effects through explicit recovery. Generic metadata effect flags alone do not prove change;
    content-only upload/clear/draw work emits no metadata event and does not advance metadata/storage revisions.
  ☐ Advance MetadataRevision once per actual published transition, including availability and retirement;
    advance StorageRevision only for established or conservatively recovered replacement. Preserve existing
    executable-generation ownership and update all mapped revision readers in this same unit.
  ☐ Make snapshot assignment manager-internal and rename HandleMetadataChanged to express assignment only.
    Commit canonical facts, availability, revisions and dependency associations; assign wrapper snapshots
    and selected-image projections before consumer callbacks. Update every caller directly.
  ☐ Reconcile affected dependency chains through the same classification path before their consumer callbacks.
    Cover buffer/renderbuffer-backed textures, view sources and selected images. Propagate original backing
    storage/availability even when image dimensions match. Preserve source identity; never resolve a stale
    association through a reused native name or acquire facts merely because publication occurred.
  ☐ Migrate normal publication, refresh, creation, managed operation completion, intercepted single calls
    and ProgramFields assignments together. Finalize a committed operation before consumer notifications;
    callback failure cannot make disposal withdraw a successful commit or delete its new allocation.
    Deliver one change per affected recipient per transition, including aliases of the initiating owner.
  ☐ Preserve weak owner/image fallback for intentional owner replacement and context-local resources.
    Apply the same classification using existing owner snapshots, excluding entry recipients already
    delivered. Owning wrappers may follow explicit replacement; borrowers/views retain original associations.
  ☐ Migrate framebuffer attachment helpers and program preparation to the shared change callback. Preserve
    meaningful configuration changes, unavailable/recovered preparation and executable replacement.
    Carry the initiating owner through completion without a framebuffer-specific field in generic operations.
    Keep explicit resize/rebuild/import completion events even for identical selections; avoid emitting
    the same owner completion again through its metadata reaction.
  ☐ Cover empty-schema families: realization/import usability, availability and retirement for vertex arrays,
    transform feedback, samplers, program pipelines, queries, fences, semaphores and memory objects.
    Preserve shader stage/lifetime metadata. Do not duplicate backend attribute/binding/sampler state,
    shader compilation results, query execution revisions or synchronization readiness in canonical metadata.
  ☐ Unify failure withdrawal, explicit recovery, scheduled retirement, native deletion and context retirement.
    Close usability immediately; capture recipients before membership is cleared and deliver a terminal
    event once per original lifetime. Later deletion must not repeat an already delivered retirement event.
    Use existing lifecycle handoff for context-affine callbacks after off-thread disposal; never issue GL
    into a replacement context or execute context-affine consumer work on the disposing thread.
  ☐ Preserve managed same-resource reentry guards. Recheck identity, revision and availability after callbacks
    that may trigger intercepted mutation/retirement; stop obsolete delivery without overwriting newer facts.
    Native interception continues forwarding once and cannot veto calls or leak observation exceptions.
    Do not introduce asynchronous notification scheduling as a new event system.
  ☐ Attempt remaining valid recipients after a consumer failure. Preserve original operation exceptions on
    failure paths; keep observer failures contained and post-commit notification failure distinct from native
    initialization failure. Never retry native work or roll back a committed allocation due to a callback.
  ☐ Remove superseded notification entry points and call-site dispatch throughout the mapped dependency
    closure. Keep explicit owner events and backend state knowledge under their existing responsibilities;
    no framebuffer-only snapshot fields or parallel compatibility paths remain.
  ☐ Add and run delegated CPU-only tests for structural equality/hash consistency, equal republication,
    initial/missing recipient delivery, explicit equal-descriptor storage/executable effects, revision rules,
    availability recovery, callback order, reentry, recipient failures, retirement deduplication and name reuse.
    Use controlled context/lifecycle inputs and cover empty-schema resources without native discovery.
  ☐ Run focused GPU integration cases for observed native mutation, same-shape storage replacement,
    same-interface executable replacement, backing identity/name reuse and retained explicit owner events.
    Verify managed and intercepted paths agree, no redundant acquisition occurs, and native dispatch is once.
  ☐ Completion: all mapped producers/consumers use the new contract, superseded routes are removed,
    required behavior is exercised and source compiles. Any coupled omission keeps this entire unit open.

Phase 3: Reconcile contracts and qualify completion
  Depends on Phase 2.
  Outcome: the implemented system and its focused evidence satisfy parent Phase 9.2.

  ☐ Update Rendering.GpuResources.md, Rendering.GpuCommands.Contracts.md and relevant sections of
    Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md to describe actual publication, equality, revisions, delivery order,
    failures, owner events and lifecycle behavior. Reconcile the governing RHI proposal where required;
    permanent architecture documents describe current code rather than migration history.
  ☐ Update Rendering.GpuCommands.Migrations.md with removed routes, final callers and exact focused
    verification evidence. Remove superseded planning notes or any temporary migration artifacts introduced
    by this work; retain useful regression tests. No additional review-report files are required.
  ☐ Perform a second source/requirement review, checking every resource family and every proposal rule
    against implementation, callers and tests. Confirm no native queries, duplicated registry, public event
    bus, new generation token, compatibility shim or unintended content notification was introduced.
  ☐ Run delegated canonical builds and required focused tests in Debug and ordinary Release. Reuse
    applicable evidence from Phase 2; rerun only what changes or unresolved findings invalidate.
    CPU evidence proves manager semantics; GPU evidence proves native integration. Neither proves live
    rendering acceptance. Do not initiate a full-suite/package/live run under this plan.
  ☐ Apply the independent audit-stage-completion workflow against the complete proposal, this plan,
    parent 9.2 and controlling documents. Resolve confirmed issues and evidence gaps, rechecking affected
    dependencies before marking any implementation or completion requirement satisfied.
  ☐ Reconcile parent Rendering.GpuCommands.todo Phase 9.2 after successful review/audit. Leave parent
    9.3 integrated/package/live work and Phase 10 test-suite cleanup open; do not promote historical
    candidate receipts into qualification of this implementation.
  ☐ Completion: source, documents and focused evidence agree; every required proposal behavior is
    implemented and independently reviewed, with no unresolved coverage or architecture gap.

Final acceptance
  ☐ All three phases are complete and every proposal requirement maps to current source and evidence.
  ☐ Every resource kind uses the same publication contract; specific reactions preserve their ownership.
  ☐ Equal snapshots cause no spurious changes; storage/executable replacement and lifecycle transitions
    remain observable even when descriptor values match.
  ☐ Snapshot/dependency consistency precedes callbacks; failures and reentry cannot undo committed work,
    starve remaining valid recipients or resurrect retired/reused identities.
  ☐ No superseded routes or migration shims remain. Parent 9.3 and Phase 10 retain their distinct scope.

Proposal coverage map
  Current-route and family inventory -> Phase 1; complete replacement -> Phase 2.
  One completion result, equality, change/revision classification -> Phase 2 value and classification tasks.
  Delivery order, dependency propagation, local owners and explicit owner events -> Phase 2 delivery tasks.
  Failure, reentry, retirement and off-thread handoff -> Phase 2 lifecycle tasks.
  CPU/native verification and no-shim closure -> Phase 2 verification; Phase 3 contracts and independent audit.
